        "subtitle": "Why friendship matters, to people and to politicians",
        "text": "A  It lifts hearts and lengthens lives. It has been praised as the ultimate good by philosophers and promoted by major religions. The wonderful good in question is friendship. Aristotle's highest goal for men and the third plank of the French Revolution (liberty, equality, fraternity), friendship is as old as humanity and as important as love or justice. But while bookshop shelves groan with self-help books on finding the perfect partner and philosophical texts on the nature of freedom, friendship barely gets a mention among academics or policy-makers.\n\nB  Friendship is the invisible thread running through society, yet its importance in our lives is, if anything, increasing. The claim that 'friends are the new family' is an overstatement, but it is certainly true that friendships play a large part both in the lives people actually lead and in the lives they hope to lead. Television programmes such as *Friends* show a world in which close friendships shape the participants' lives: parents and children are allowed, at best, walk-on parts.\n\nC  One of the reasons why thinkers struggle to recognise this trend may be a matter of definition. After all, I am a friend to someone I have known and loved for twenty-five years, but I am also a 'Friend of the Earth'. Friendship is a slippery category. This, however, is where philosophers are supposed to help. Aristotle divided friendships into three types: friendships for usefulness, friendships for pleasure and friendships of virtue.\n\nD  The first kind of friend is the one who will get you a job or membership of an exclusive club; the second makes you laugh. But in both cases the point of the friendship is that the friend provides something of separate value to you. True friendship, the third kind, is valued for itself. There are few limits on the number of friends of the first two kinds (I can have a vast business network and hundreds of pleasant acquaintances), but true friendship is, by definition, a limited field: if someone has many friends, they have none.\n\nE  Virtuous friendship is long-term and committed and brings great psychological benefits, and there is plenty of research evidence showing that having at least one close friend is linked to a range of health benefits, from quicker recovery from heart illness to fewer mental health problems.\n\nF  However, friendship is not always a pure good. Its benefits are unevenly spread, and its effect on traditional liberal values, such as equality, diversity and mobility, is mixed. The first problem is that men are worse at friendship than women. It is now widely accepted that women do more of the 'social' work than men and have better-developed friendship skills, which leaves men at a disadvantage.\n\nG  Secondly, friendship has political downsides for governments that are committed to social inclusion: it is, by definition, exclusive. People from a particular social class or educational background are very likely to form friendships, or romantic relationships, with people from the same background. Since friends help each other, the danger is that the friendships of the wealthy and successful keep social advantage to themselves, at the expense of social mobility.\n\nH  Friendship is seen, with some justification, as a private matter, but the strong links between friendship and other social goods, including better health, more successful careers and greater life satisfaction, should be enough to earn it more attention from decision-makers. So what are the political implications of these observations? Perhaps the best that politicians can aim for is not to make things worse for friendship. But political institutions can improve or worsen the conditions in which friendships are formed.\n\nI  First of all, the question of time needs to be considered. Friendship needs time to flourish: Aristotle reckoned that one and a half bushels of salt had to be eaten together before a friendship became solid. At present, many people regard their hours outside work as family time, but little allowance is made for the time needed to build friendships. Ironically, it could be counter-productive for politicians to discourage people from working long hours, since a third of us make most of our friends through work. What decision-makers could do, however, is discourage the drive for commercial profit that squeezes friendliness out of the workplace.\n\nJ  There is also a case for encouraging spaces where people from different backgrounds meet and interact, to increase the chances of friendships across social classes. With growing geographical inequality, as rich people increasingly live in neighbourhoods of rich people, only hospital wards and places of worship are sites of genuine social mixing.\n\nK  Friendship is a virtue with some of the appearance of a social vice. It can promote or hold back social mobility, support tolerance or strengthen discrimination, wear away hierarchies or keep them in place. A society could be made up of strong friendships between people of identical social backgrounds who treat everyone else with contempt, intolerance or fear. The true test of the friendliness of a community is not simply the way its citizens treat their friends, but whether they behave generously towards the wider social world. We need not only the care of friends, but the kindness of strangers."
